# EXT-17 — line endings, declared rather than inherited.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (F-42). Git for Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, and until this
# file was added there was nothing to override it, so what a clone contained depended on the
# cloner's git configuration. Measured on a cold clone of `master`:
#
#     contract/capture-atacama-air-defense-sample.n8rocap.jsonl   0 CR here, 7180 CR in a clone
#     contract/example.conditions.json                            0 CR here,   78 CR in a clone
#
# It was invisible from the working directory. Those files were written LF by the vendoring and
# never checked out again, so the working copy stayed correct while every clone did not. Only a
# clone reads what ships.
#
# WHAT IT BROKE, PRECISELY — because it is narrower than it looks. Not the tests:
# `CaptureReader.cpp` strips a stray CR rather than making it fatal (format §2) and
# `JsonParse.cpp` treats one as whitespace, so both files still parse and all 475 checks still
# pass on a fresh clone. What it broke is the thing `contract/` exists to be. E-5 was resolved by
# EXT-08 creating a real upstream file that this project vendors **by identity**, and CLAUDE.md
# says so in those words. A checkout entitled to rewrite 7180 of its bytes means the identity
# held on one machine and nowhere else. A pin that only pins locally is not a pin.
#
# ORDERING MATTERS AND IS THE EASY MISTAKE. In a `.gitattributes` the LAST matching line wins,
# so the general defaults come first and the specific pins come last. Written the other way
# round — pins first, `*.json text` last — `*.json` would silently un-pin
# `contract/example.conditions.json` and this file would look right while doing nothing.

# --- Defaults ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sources and documents are ordinary text: normalised to LF in the repository, checked out in
# whatever the platform wants. Nothing compares any of them byte for byte.
*.cpp   text
*.h     text
*.md    text
*.json  text
*.jsonl text
*.py    text
*.txt   text
*.log   text
*.yml   text

# cmd.exe is documented to want CRLF and misparses some constructs without it. Declared rather
# than left to autocrlf, so a clone on a machine configured `core.autocrlf=false` — or on Linux,
# where this repository is browsed even though nothing here runs — still gets batch files that
# work when copied to a Windows box.
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# --- CRLF pins: artifacts a Windows tool wrote and a Windows tool compares ----------------------
# These are stored LF in the repository (they always have been) and checked out CRLF EVERYWHERE,
# rather than only on a machine whose autocrlf happens to be `true`. That is the point: the
# goldens are compared with `fc` against what the binaries actually print, and a Windows console
# program prints CRLF. A reviewer cloning with `core.autocrlf=false` — a common setting — would
# otherwise get LF goldens, and the build would fail on a difference nobody introduced.
help.golden.txt         text eol=crlf
tests/checks.golden.txt text eol=crlf

# `n8ro-judge --verify` byte-compares a re-judgement against the live run's verdicts, so the
# committed verdicts must check out as the tool will write them.
verdicts*.jsonl         text eol=crlf

# Every campaign's run records, report, run-to-run diff and transcript: the artifacts a number in
# a milestone document traces to. All were written by a Windows tool; all check out as written.
campaigns/**            text eol=crlf

# --- Byte-exact pins: LAST, because the last matching line wins ---------------------------------
# `-text` means git stores and restores these bytes exactly and converts nothing, in either
# direction, on any platform. These two lines are at the bottom of the file deliberately: a
# capture may live under `campaigns/`, and the CRLF pin above must not be allowed to reach it.

# All of `contract/`, not only the data files. The two specifications are vendored by identity
# too, and a drift check is worth nothing if checkout may edit its input.
contract/**     -text

# Any capture, anywhere — including one produced by a run into a campaign directory. The format's
# §2 is "LF. Never CRLF, on any platform". This is that sentence enforced against the one process
# in the pipeline that was ignoring it.
*.n8rocap.jsonl -text
